## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic: every rate integrated
against the game time each frame advances by, so an interval of game time reaches
the same state however it was divided into frames. Two more properties make the
game driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from the elapsed time the game is handed,
  independent of a canvas, of the frame loop that measured it, and of wall-clock
  time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads nothing from the
  renderer. Whether a tile is blocked, which unit an emitter targets, how much a
  shot removes, and how a frame's heat resolves are all decided from the floor
  alone.
- Seeded randomness. Any randomness the game uses runs off a generator seeded
  from the state's generator field, and it keeps its whole generator state in
  that field, so reseeding and replaying the same calls reproduces the same
  result exactly. The vent each unit enters at is drawn from it.

Given the same seed and the same sequence of calls and elapsed game time, the
game reaches the same state every time.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard, the pointer's own hardware events, and the overlay belong to the
runtime layer beneath the game, and the surface carries no operation for the
keyboard or the overlay. That layer reports which keys are down and draws and
toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `advance` below.

Muting is the other. There is no operation that sets it: `mute` is reached the
way a player reaches it, through its binding in `specs/controls.md` or the
panel's mute control, and the snapshot reports the result.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, the audio bus, and the overlay belong to the
{{engine.name}} engine, which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the
root of this repository, and the surface carries no operation for any of them.
The engine advances the game frame by frame and can take it off real time, it
drives the actions the game registers, it owns muting, and it draws and toggles
the debug overlay.

There is therefore no operation that sets muting: `mute` is reached the way a
player reaches it, through its binding in `specs/controls.md` or the panel's mute
control, and the snapshot reports the result.
{{/if}}

The pointer is on the surface, because a scripted scenario presses the panel and
the floor the way a player does. What the overlay shows is Meltdown's, under
Diagnostics below.

## Identity

Every tower and every surge unit carries an `id`: a number, distinct among the
entities live at any moment, reported by `snapshot` and taken by every
per-entity operation. Two rules make an id findable without an assignment
scheme:

- An entity added through this surface is appended to its roster, so it is the
  last entry and its id is read from there. A tower committed by `place` is
  appended the same way.
- An id is never reused while the entity holding it is live, and an entity keeps
  its id for its whole life.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`MELTDOWN_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number,
and the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, and booleans. `col`
and `row` are tile coordinates as `specs/floor.md` defines them, and every `x`
and `y` the surface takes or reports is an entity's centre in the stage's logical
units, so a caller aiming at a tile passes that tile's centre,
`(18 + 19c + 9.5, 18 + 19r + 9.5)`.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}

Each operation takes the current state, `DeepReadonly<MeltdownState>`, as its
first parameter. A pose returns the next `MeltdownState`, built from the one it
was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read. The
signatures below name the parameters that follow the state, so
`setTowerHeat(id, heat)` below is `setTowerHeat(state, id, heat)` on the surface
and `snapshot()` is `snapshot(state)`.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "structured-2d")}}

Each operation takes only the parameters its signature names and acts on the live
game the moment it is called. A pose returns nothing; a reading returns what it
read and changes nothing.
{{/if}}

### The core

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `reset(options)` | Restores every declared field of the state to its title-screen value, listed below. |
| `snapshot()` | A pure read of the state, returned as the object under Snapshot shape below. It changes nothing. |

`reset` restores `screen` to `"title"`, `phase` to `"opening"`, `menuIndex` to
`0`, `mode` to `"containment"`, `difficulty` to `"medium"`, `money` to the
starting money that pair gives, `lives` to its starting lives, `score` to `0`,
`wave` to `1`, `buildTimer` to `0`, `wavePending` to `0`, `speed` to `1`,
`selected` to `null`, `hoverShop` to `null`, `build` to `null`, and `simTime` to
`0`. It empties the tower and surge rosters, clears the spawner's clock, and
turns the world gate `waveSpawning` back on.

`options.seed`, a number defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`), seeds all of the
game's randomness. `muted` is left exactly as it stands, because muting is a
player preference the runtime owns, and so is `pointer`, which mirrors something
the runtime owns as well.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
`autoStep` is left as it stands too: who drives the clock is not part of the
game's state.

### The clock

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setAutoStep(enabled)` | Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. |
| `advance(seconds, frames)` | Runs whole frames of game time immediately. |

`setAutoStep(false)` stops the frame loop advancing the simulation from the wall
clock, so the game changes only when `advance` says so. `setAutoStep(true)`
returns it to running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played.
Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent frame left. It changes no game state, and is reported
as `autoStep`.

`advance(seconds, frames)` runs `frames` whole frames covering `seconds` of game
time, each worth `seconds / frames`, immediately and in order. `frames` defaults
to `1`. Each is a real frame, the same update the loop runs followed by a render,
so the game's own rules produce the result and the canvas reflects it.

Every rate in this game is per second and integrated against the frame's game
time, so `advance(1, 1)` and `advance(1, 120)` cover the same second and reach
the same outcome, beyond the drift a change in step size explains.
{{/if}}

### The screen and the run

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setScreen(screen)` | Sets the current screen: `"title"`, `"modeselect"`, `"difficultyselect"`, `"howto"`, `"playing"`, `"paused"`, `"victory"`, or `"gameover"`. |
| `setPhase(phase)` | Sets the sub-phase of the `playing` screen: `"opening"`, `"building"`, or `"wave"`. |
| `setMenuIndex(n)` | Sets the highlighted row of whatever menu the current screen shows, counted from `0`. |
| `setMode(mode)` | Sets the mode: `"containment"`, `"hundred"`, `"deeppockets"`, `"bottleneck"`, or `"suddendeath"`. |
| `setDifficulty(difficulty)` | Sets the difficulty: `"easy"`, `"medium"`, or `"hard"`. |
| `setMoney(amount)` | Sets the current money. |
| `setLives(count)` | Sets the lives remaining. |
| `setScore(n)` | Sets the score. |
| `setWave(n)` | Sets the current wave number. |
| `setBuildTimer(seconds)` | Sets the seconds left in the current build phase. |
| `setWavePending(n)` | Sets how many units of the current wave are still to be released. |
| `setSpeed(speed)` | Sets the game-speed toggle, `1` or `2`. |

`setScreen` and `setPhase` set that field alone and run no entry effect. Neither
resets a menu index, pays interest or a wave-clear bonus, grants a score, changes
any tower's freshness, releases or clears a wave, or plays a cue. Reaching an
entry effect is what the run's own transitions are for.

`setMode` and `setDifficulty` change no other field. The derived figures of
`specs/modes.md`, `waveCount`, `startMoney`, `startLives`, `interest`, and
`buildZone`, follow them, and the run's live money, lives, and wave do not.

`setLives` triggers no game over: the loss belongs to the leak path, and this is
a precondition. `setScore` pays no bonus and grants nothing, whatever boundary it
carries the score across. `setWave` rebuilds nothing, releases nothing, and
clears nothing; the coming wave's type and count and the hp scaling follow it.

### The world gate

| Operation | Gates |
| --- | --- |
| `setWaveSpawning(enabled)` | The run's own release of surge: the build timer's automatic start of the next wave when it reaches `0`, and the spawner's release of the units counted by `wavePending`. Off, no unit arrives unless one is added, and a build timer driven to `0` leaves the phase where it stands. The timer still counts down, a unit already on the floor still walks, and everything else runs on. It is on by default, restored to on by `reset`, and reported as `waveSpawning`. |

### The towers

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addTower(type, col, row, rotation)` | Adds one tower of `type` with its footprint's top-left at tile `(col, row)` and its placement `rotation` (`0` to `3`). It costs nothing, spends nothing, and runs no placement check. The footprint's tiles become blocked and the routes are recomputed exactly as a placed tower's are. The tower starts at heat `0`, level `1`, not tripped, with `spent` equal to its build cost, `fresh` true, and both faculties on. It is appended to `towers`. |
| `removeTower(id)` | Removes that tower. Its footprint reopens and the routes are recomputed. It pays no refund and changes neither money nor score. |
| `clearTowers()` | Removes every tower, reopening every footprint and recomputing the routes. It pays no refund and changes neither money nor score, and it leaves the surge standing. |
| `setTowerHeat(id, heat)` | Sets the emitter's heat, a number in `[0, 100]`. It does not trip the tower: the trip belongs to the heat model, and this is a precondition. |
| `setTowerTripped(id, tripped)` | Sets whether the tower is offline on a trip. It sets that flag alone, touching neither the heat nor `tripTimer` and raising no cue. |
| `setTowerTripTimer(id, seconds)` | Sets the seconds left on the trip cooldown. It sets that field alone and does not trip or un-trip the tower. |
| `setTowerLevel(id, level)` | Sets the tower's level, `1`, `2`, or `3`. It spends nothing, and every stat `specs/towers.md` derives from the level follows. |
| `setTowerFresh(id, fresh)` | Sets whether the tower still refunds in full. |
| `setTowerFiring(id, enabled)` | The firing gate, below. |
| `setTowerThermal(id, enabled)` | The thermal gate, below. |

Two faculty gates hold one faculty of one tower, and each gates that faculty and
nothing else. Both are on for a tower the game itself placed, and both are
reported by `snapshot`.

| Gate | What it holds |
| --- | --- |
| `firingEnabled` | The tower's targeting and firing: acquiring a target, resolving a shot, the damage and splash the shot deals, the slow a Rime applies, and the `heatPerShot` the shot adds. Off, the tower's thermal model runs exactly as an idle tower's does: it cools, conducts, exchanges with movers, and trips. |
| `thermalEnabled` | The tower's part in the heat model: its air cooling, its conduction with its neighbours, the flow a Forge or a Sink drives into or out of it, and its trip. Off, an emitter's heat holds exactly where it was posed while it goes on acquiring targets, firing at its rate, and dealing its damage at that pinned heat. |

There is no operation that rotates a placed tower. Orientation is fixed at
placement, so `rotation` is set by `addTower` and by the preview's rotation at
the moment `place` commits.

### Building

`setArmed`, `setPreview`, and `setPreviewRotation` pose the held preview.
`place`, `upgradeTower`, and `sellTower` are single things a player does, running
through the game's own code, so each has every consequence
`specs/building.md` gives it.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `setArmed(type)` | Arms placement for a tower `type`, exactly as choosing its shop entry does. `null` disarms. |
| `setPreview(col, row)` | Moves the held preview so its footprint's top-left sits at tile `(col, row)`, clamped so the footprint stays fully on the grid. `build.valid` follows through the real placement check. |
| `setPreviewRotation(rotation)` | Sets the held rotation, `0` to `3`. |
| `place()` | Commits the held preview at its current footprint if `build.valid` is true, exactly as a press on the floor does. On an invalid footprint it builds nothing and spends nothing. |
| `setSelected(id)` | Selects the placed tower with that id, opening its inspector. `null` deselects. |
| `setHoverShop(type)` | Sets the shop entry currently hovered, so the panel shows that type's info. `null` clears the hover. |
| `upgradeTower(id)` | Upgrades that tower one level through the real upgrade code, deducting its `upgradeCost` when it is affordable and the tower is below level `3`, and changing nothing otherwise. |
| `sellTower(id)` | Sells that tower through the real sell code: it pays its `refund` into money, removes it, reopens its footprint, recomputes the routes, and clears the selection if it was selected. |

No operation on this surface plays a cue, and none can. A cue is raised by the
frame that resolves the event it answers and is played from the frame loop, and
an operation of this surface resolves outside that frame. Every cue is reached
the way a player reaches it.

There is no operation that asks whether a footprint could be placed.
`build.valid` answers that through the same check, and it is the value the player
sees on the preview.

### The surge

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `addUnit(type, vent)` | Adds one unit of `type` (`"mote"`, `"sprint"`, `"hulk"`, `"swarm"`, `"drift"`, or `"core"`) at `vent` (`"left"` or `"top"`), entering it into the same pathing and combat systems the spawner uses. Its exhaust is that vent's fixed opposite, its `maxHp` is its base hp scaled for the current wave, and it counts toward `waveRemaining` while the phase is `wave`. It is appended to `surge`. |
| `removeUnit(id)` | Removes that unit. It costs no life, pays no bounty, and changes neither score nor money. |
| `clearSurge()` | Removes every unit, leaving the towers standing with their heat, levels, and tallies untouched. It costs no life, pays no bounty, and changes neither score nor money. |
| `setUnitPosition(id, x, y)` | Places the unit's centre at a logical stage position. Its route is recomputed from the tile that position falls in; its vent and exhaust are unchanged. |
| `setUnitHp(id, hp)` | Sets the unit's current hp. It does not kill the unit: death belongs to the damage path. |
| `setUnitMaxHp(id, maxHp)` | Sets the unit's maximum hp, which its health bar is drawn against. |
| `setUnitSlow(id, factor)` | Sets the fraction of speed the live slow removes, `0` for none. `speed` follows immediately. |
| `setUnitSlowTimer(id, seconds)` | Sets the seconds the live slow has left, `0` for none. |
| `setUnitMotion(id, enabled)` | The motion gate, below. |

`motion` gates the unit's locomotion, which is whether it advances along its
route or its flight line, and nothing else. Off, the unit holds its position, and
its route is still computed from the tile it stands on, so `remaining` still
follows the floor and rises when a wall is built across its way. It is `true` for
a unit the game itself released.

A unit's flight and its vent are fixed by its type and by where it entered, so
the surface poses neither.

### The pointer

These feed the same pointer input path the runtime feeds, so a posed press and a
player's press are the same event. Positions are logical stage coordinates.

| Operation | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `pointerDown(x, y)` | Reports a press at that position. |
| `pointerMove(x, y)` | Reports a move to that position. |
| `pointerUp()` | Reports a release at the last reported position. |

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns exactly this object. Every field an operation can set is
present, so every operation is verifiable by setting a value and reading it back.

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "modeselect" | "difficultyselect" | "howto"
        | "playing" | "paused" | "victory" | "gameover",
  phase: "opening" | "building" | "wave",   // the sub-phase of "playing"
  menuIndex: <number>,                      // highlighted row, from 0
  mode: "containment" | "hundred" | "deeppockets" | "bottleneck" | "suddendeath",
  difficulty: "easy" | "medium" | "hard",
  money: <number>,
  lives: <number>,
  score: <number>,
  wave: <number>,                           // the wave this phase belongs to
  waveCount: <number>,                      // derived from mode and difficulty
  startMoney: <number>,                     // derived
  startLives: <number>,                     // derived
  interest: <boolean>,                      // derived: this mode pays interest
  buildTimer: <number>,                     // seconds left; 0 in the opening phase
  wavePending: <number>,                    // units of this wave still to release
  waveRemaining: <number>,                  // derived: wavePending + live wave units
  nextWave: { type: <surgeType>, count: <number> } | null,
  speed: 1 | 2,
  muted: <boolean>,                         // the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit
  waveSpawning: <boolean>,                  // the world gate
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
  autoStep: <boolean>,                      // who drives the clock
{{/if}}
  pointer: { x: <number>, y: <number>,      // the pointer's own position, in logical
             down: <boolean> },             // stage units, and whether it is pressed
  selected: <number> | null,
  hoverShop: <towerType> | null,
  build: {                                  // the held preview, else null
    type: <towerType>,
    col: <number>, row: <number>,           // the footprint's top-left tile
    rotation: 0 | 1 | 2 | 3,
    valid: <boolean>,
  } | null,
  buildZone: { col0: <number>, row0: <number>,
               col1: <number>, row1: <number> } | null,   // inclusive; null off Bottleneck
  paths: {
    // The cheapest open route from each vent's opening to its opposite exhaust's
    // opening, in tiles, under the surge's own step rules.
    left: { length: <number> },             // left vent to right exhaust
    top:  { length: <number> },             // top vent to bottom exhaust
  },
  // Every control the build panel offers, as its hit rectangle in logical stage
  // units, so a scripted scenario operates the panel the build laid out.
  controls: {
    shop: [ { type: <towerType>, x, y, w, h }, ... ],  // one per entry, in shop order
    rotate:  { x, y, w, h } | null,         // null when no placement is armed
    cancel:  { x, y, w, h } | null,
    upgrade: { x, y, w, h } | null,         // null when no tower is selected
    sell:    { x, y, w, h } | null,
    send:    { x, y, w, h },
    speed:   { x, y, w, h },
    pause:   { x, y, w, h },
    mute:    { x, y, w, h },
  },
  towers: [
    {
      id: <number>,
      type: <towerType>,
      col: <number>, row: <number>,         // footprint top-left tile
      size: 2 | 3 | 4,
      rotation: 0 | 1 | 2 | 3,
      level: 1 | 2 | 3,
      heat: <number>,                       // 0..100; 0 for the Forge and Sink
      redline: <number>,
      heatMult: <number>,                   // live multiplier; 0 for movers only
      damage: <number>,                     // per-shot damage; 0 for movers only
      slowFactor: <number>,                 // the Rime's live slow fraction; 0 otherwise
      output: <number>,                     // the Forge's setpoint or the Sink's per-edge
                                            // cooling at this level; 0 for emitters
      tripped: <boolean>,
      tripTimer: <number>,                  // seconds left on the cooldown, else 0
      firing: <boolean>,                    // has a target and is online this frame;
                                            // always false while tripped
      targeting: <number> | null,           // the id of the unit it is firing on
      radiatorFaces: [ "N" | "E" | "S" | "W", ... ],   // world-oriented; empty for movers
      kills: <number>,
      damageDealt: <number>,
      spent: <number>,                      // total money spent on this tower
      refund: <number>,                     // what selling it pays right now
      upgradeCost: <number>,                // 0 at level 3
      fresh: <boolean>,                     // still refunds in full
      firingEnabled: <boolean>,
      thermalEnabled: <boolean>,
    },
  ],
  surge: [
    {
      id: <number>,
      type: <surgeType>,
      x: <number>, y: <number>,             // the unit's CENTRE
      col: <number>, row: <number>,         // the tile its centre falls in
      hp: <number>, maxHp: <number>,
      speed: <number>,                      // current speed, reflecting any slow
      baseSpeed: <number>,                  // its unslowed speed
      slowed: <boolean>,
      slowFactor: <number>,                 // the fraction of speed the slow removes
      slowTimer: <number>,                  // seconds the slow has left
      flying: <boolean>,
      vent: "left" | "top",
      exhaust: "right" | "bottom",
      remaining: <number>,                  // route length still to travel, in tiles
      motion: <boolean>,
    },
  ],
  simTime: <number>,                        // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
}
```

Notes on the shape:

- Every `(x, y)` this surface reports or accepts is the entity's centre.
- `waveCount`, `startMoney`, `startLives`, `interest`, and `buildZone` are
  derived from `mode` and `difficulty` by the table in `specs/modes.md`.
- `waveRemaining` is `wavePending` plus the number of live units while the phase
  is `wave`, and `wavePending` alone otherwise.
- `nextWave` reports the coming wave, which is the wave a build or opening phase
  is preparing for, `wave`, and during the `wave` phase the wave after the one
  being fought, `wave + 1`. It is `null` when that number falls outside `1`
  through `waveCount`, so a run has no coming wave while its last one is being
  fought. Its `type` and `count` are the type and the size `specs/waves.md` gives
  that wave. In The Hundred, whose one onslaught fields more than one type,
  `count` is `HUNDRED_UNITS` (`100`) and `type` is the type of the onslaught's
  first unit, which is the Mote.
- `paths` is never `null`, because the floor can never be sealed, so a wall that
  lengthens a route shows up in its length.
- `remaining` is measured under the surge's own step rules, an orthogonal step
  costing `1` tile and a diagonal `sqrt(2)`; for a flyer it is the straight-line
  distance to its exhaust divided by the tile size.
- `radiatorFaces` are reported in world orientation, so they name the faces that
  point outward after the tower's placement rotation.
- A unit's `baseSpeed` is its type's speed and its `speed` is that base reduced
  by any live slow. `slowed` is true exactly when `slowFactor` is above `0`.
- `controls` reports where the build panel put each control, refreshed at the
  call. It is a diagnostic read: no operation sets it and nothing in the game
  depends on it.
- `pointer` mirrors the position the pointer input reports and whether the
  pointer is currently pressed, refreshed in every update. It is the pointer's
  own position, never an entity's centre.
- `muted` is the game's copy of the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every
  update, so the snapshot reports the bit the runtime holds. It is not read at
  the call. No operation sets it; the `mute` action and the panel's mute control
  do.
- A tower's `size`, `redline`, `refund`, and `upgradeCost` are the figures
  `specs/towers.md` and `specs/building.md` give it at its current level.
  `heatMult` is `heatMultiplier(heat, redline)`, `damage` is the per-shot damage
  `specs/combat.md` states, `slowFactor` is a Rime's `slowFactor(heat)` at its
  level and `0` on every other tower, and `output` is a mover's figure at its
  level.
- `heatMult` and `damage` are `0` for the Forge and the Sink and for nothing
  else. The Rime reports both like any other emitter, because it is one.
- `simTime` accumulates the game time the simulation advanced by, so it holds
  still while the game is paused and gains at twice the rate at `speed` `2`.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
- `autoStep` reports which side is driving the clock.
{{/if}}

## Diagnostics

The debug overlay is read-only, never changes gameplay, and shows the values the
game registers with it as diagnostic sources. Register at least:

- the current `screen` and `phase`;
- the mode and the difficulty;
- the money, the lives, the wave, and the score;
- the lengths of the two routes;
- for each tower, its id, its type, its level, its heat, its redline, whether it
  is tripped, and its kills;
- for each surge unit, its id, its type, the tile it stands on, its hp, and
  whether it is slowed.

Keep each one short enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read,
so watching the overlay leaves the game exactly as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key as `specs/controls.md`
states, it is off when the game starts, and it reads the game without changing
it.
{{else if (eq engine.slug "simple-2d")}}
Registering those values is the whole of Meltdown's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the read,
as `DeepReadonly<MeltdownState>`, and reads off that argument. Drawing the panel,
toggling it, and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Meltdown's part, through
`world.diagnostics` in the game mode's `beginPlay`. Each source is a function of
no arguments, called at each read; read the live state at the call, through the
world the mode holds, so the panel reports the frame being drawn. Drawing the
panel and toggling it are the engine's.
{{/if}}
